WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.700
The first film tilted the per-capita line by assumption.

2
00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:12.120
In a real forest or on a real reef, how would you catch that line tilting?

3
00:00:13.183 --> 00:00:15.703
Start in the Yukon, near Kluane Lake.

4
00:00:17.163 --> 00:00:20.743
Snowshoe hares there rise and fall in a cycle of roughly a decade.

5
00:00:22.567 --> 00:00:25.767
Charles Krebs and colleagues worked on blocks of forest.

6
00:00:27.067 --> 00:00:30.887
They added food, fenced out mammal predators, did both, or did neither.

7
00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:36.830
During the cycle's peak and decline, fencing out mammal predators raised hare

8
00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:38.730
density, and so did food.

9
00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:42.370
Doing both raised it far more than either alone.

10
00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:46.130
So food and predators both limited the hares.

11
00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:49.810
But were individuals doing worse at high density?

12
00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:52.490
Counts of hares cannot say.

13
00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:57.450
For that you need a per-capita rate, measured at several densities.

14
00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:02.770
Mark Hixon and Mark Carr did this on small patch reefs in the Bahamas.

15
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.020
A per-capita measure counts outcomes per individual, not heads.

16
00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:13.720
Here it is the fraction of young fish that died during the study on each reef.

17
00:01:14.783 --> 00:01:20.143
They stocked newly settled blue chromis, a small reef fish, at different densities.

18
00:01:21.443 --> 00:01:23.963
Some reefs got few young fish, some got many.

19
00:01:25.033 --> 00:01:27.333
They also crossed two kinds of predator.

20
00:01:29.073 --> 00:01:30.073
Residents live on the reef.

21
00:01:31.353 --> 00:01:34.413
Transients swim in from open water and move between reefs.

22
00:01:35.967 --> 00:01:40.547
Then they plotted the fraction of young fish that died against starting density.

23
00:01:41.807 --> 00:01:45.227
A fraction dying is a per-capita measure, so it is teal.

24
00:01:46.217 --> 00:01:49.677
With both predator groups present, the line rose steeply.

25
00:01:50.797 --> 00:01:54.917
The more young fish that settled on a reef, the larger the share that died.

26
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.500
That rising line is density-dependent mortality.

27
00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:05.120
Predators caused it on these reefs, at least when both kinds hunted there together.

28
00:02:06.633 --> 00:02:10.033
Now take away one of the two predator groups, either one.

29
00:02:11.193 --> 00:02:14.033
Does the teal line still rise as density goes up?

30
00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:23.330
With only one group, no rise was detected.

31
00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:29.610
But one of those tests had low statistical power, too little data to catch a real

32
00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:34.230
slope reliably. Not detected is not the same as absent.

33
00:02:35.333 --> 00:02:38.473
Notice what made this a test of density dependence.

34
00:02:39.733 --> 00:02:43.373
Not more deaths in total, but a larger fraction of the fish dying.

35
00:02:44.517 --> 00:02:47.897
The hares and the chromis answer two different questions.

36
00:02:49.257 --> 00:02:53.137
Adding food and removing predators showed what limited the hares.

37
00:02:54.197 --> 00:02:59.077
The chromis showed density dependence, within one generation and across reefs.

38
00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:02.700
A brake is negative density dependence.

39
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.100
As density rises, deaths rise or births fall, so net per-capita growth falls.

40
00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.920
You find it only by measuring per-capita rates.
